data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"**Recommendation: keep `mode` as an explicit dependency.** Don't have `alerts.ts` import runtime config directly. Read `emergencyMode` once at the worker's entry point and pass it in.\n\n**Why**\n• `alerts` stays pure and predictable: the same input always gives the same behavior, and there's no hidden global state.\n• `config.ts` stays the one place that knows about env vars and parsing. `alerts` only knows \"normal vs emergency.\"\n• You avoid problems with import order or stale values if config ever loads lazily or changes at runtime.\n• Tests don't need module mocks.\n\n**Shape**\n• If many call sites pass `mode`, use a factory instead: `createAlerts({ emergencyMode })`, called once at the entry point.\n• If `emergencyMode` can change while the worker runs, inject a getter (`() => boolean`) rather than a snapshot.\n• Use a narrow type (`boolean` or `'normal' | 'emergency'`), not the whole config object.\n\n**Test strategy**\n• **`alerts` unit tests:** run the same cases against both modes in one table and check what's sent, suppressed or escalated. Pass the mode directly and don't mock the config module.\n• **`config` unit tests:** cover parsing `emergencyMode` from env: the default, valid values and bad values (fail fast or fall back, whichever you choose).\n• **One wiring test at the entry point:** set the config to emergency and confirm `alerts` acts in emergency mode. This catches the one bug that explicit injection makes possible: forgetting to pass the value through.\n• **If you use a getter:** add a test that flips the value mid-run and checks that `alerts` picks up the change.\n\nImporting config directly is only reasonable if `emergencyMode` is a fixed, process-wide setting and `alerts` is used in a lot of places. Even then, I'd still go with the factory."}}

